idence":88,"evidenceIds":["E12","E2"]},{"itemId":"I23","answer":"sufficient","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E8","E10","E14","E15"]}],"evidenceRequests":["Q3","Q2","Q1"],"summary":"The absenteeism story fails. The 40% rise is mostly a recording artifact: ShiftLine began logging partial-day absences, which account for 238 of the 245 monthly increase, and full-day absences rose only 1.1%. The dispatch log, which is independent of ShiftLine, shows no change in unstaffed trips, late pull-outs or extraboard use. The OTP decline is real, since manual ride checks agree with the AVL. It is explained by two causes. Cutting recovery time on 21 routes stepped down OTP in early April, and the Halvorsen and Bridgeport lane reductions stepped it down again in late April on five of those routes. Group C, with neither change, is flat. Ridership and dwell time are unchanged. Recommended actions are to restore recovery time, set construction-period running times, and restate the absence data. The proposed discipline program would punish operators for an artifact."}}